                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+stack"} derived stack
                  %h2#theorems Theorems
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diaconescu%27s+theorem"} Diaconescu's theorem
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barr%27s+theorem"} Barr's theorem
                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/topos+theory+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#big_and_little_toposes Big and little toposes
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#relationships"} Relationships
              %li
                %a{:href => "#the_big_and_little_topos_of_an_object"} The big and little topos of an object
              %li
                %a{:href => "#axiomatizations"} Axiomatizations
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            There are two different (related) relationships between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topoi"} Grothendieck topoi
            and a notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                generalized
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} category of sheaves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo =
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T = Sh(S)
            on some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            On the one hand, we can regard the topos
            %em itself
            as a generalized space. This tends to be a useful point of view when the site
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+open+subsets"} category of open subsets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(X)
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (or some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            or the like), or some other site which we regard as containing data from only “one space.” In this case, we refer to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            as a
            = succeed "," do
              %strong little topos
            = succeed "." do
              %strong petit topos
          %p
            On the other hand, we can view a topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            as a well-behaved category whose
            %em objects
            are generalized spaces. This tends to be a useful point of view when the site
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is a category of
            %em
              all test
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            in some sense, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            as a
            = succeed "," do
              %strong big topos
            = succeed "." do
              %strong gros topos
          %p
            These distinctions carry over in a straightforward way to higher topoi such as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topoi"} (∞,1)-topoi
          %h2#relationships Relationships
          %p
            Objects in a big topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(S)
            may be thought of as
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                modeled on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivation+for+sheaves%2C+cohomology+and+higher+stacks"} motivation for sheaves, cohomology and higher stacks
            and at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
          %p
            On the other hand, the objects of a petit topos, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(X)
            %em
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            on which the rigid structure of morphisms in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(X)
            (only inclusions of subsets, no more general maps) induces a correspondingly rigid structure so that they are not all that general. In fact,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(Op(X))
            is equivalent to the category of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/etale+space"} etale space
            = succeed "\u2014i.e." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            can be identified with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+homeomorphisms+of+toposes"} local homeomorphisms of toposes
            into
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
          %p
            From the “little topos” perspective, it can be helpful to think of a “big topos” as a “fat point,” which is not “spread out” very much spatially itself, but contains within that point lots of different types of “local data,” so that even spaces which are “rigidly” modeled on that point can have a lot of interesting cohesion and local structure. (One should not be misled by this into thinking that a big topos has
            %em only
            one
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point+of+a+topos"} point
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+topos"} local topos
            and hence has an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial
            point.)
          %h2#the_big_and_little_topos_of_an_object The big and little topos of an object
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a topological space, then the canonical little topos associated to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is the sheaf topos
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(X)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is a site of probes enabling us to regard
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as an object of a big topos
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo =
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H = Sh(S)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H/X
            as a representative of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %strong
              little topos of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong
                petit topos of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %strong
              big topos of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong
                gros topos of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            There might be some debate about whether
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H/X
            is, itself, “a little topos” or “a big topos.” While it certainly contains information about the space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            specifically, its objects are not “spaces locally modeled on
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            which happen to have a map to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Note that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is actually an
            %em object
            of the site
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H/X
            can be identified with the topos of sheaves on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+category"} slice
            site
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S/X
            (and otherwise, it can be identified with the topos of sheaves on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+elements"} category of elements
            of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in Sh(S)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S/X
            is often referred to as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/big+site"} big site
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/little+site"} little site
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(X)
            (or appropriate replacement). The topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(S/X)
            can thus be viewed as spaces modelled on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Note that when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo =
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S=Top
            with its local-homeomorphism topology, there is a canonical functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(X) \to S/X
            which preserves finite limits and both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover-preserving+functor"} preserves
            and
            %span.newWikiWord
              reflects
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/cover-reflecting+functor"} ?
            covering families. Therefore, it induces both a geometric morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H/X \to Sh(X)
            and one
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(X) \to H/X
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topos"} Topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H/X \to Sh(X)
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+geometric+morphism"} local
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence+of+toposes"} homotopy equivalence of toposes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            also holds in other cases.
          %h2#axiomatizations Axiomatizations
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                If a site
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                is given by a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+pretopology"} Grothendieck pretopology
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/little+site"} little site
                associated to any object of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %li
              %p
                One proposed axiomatization of the notion of big topos is that of a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
            %li
              %p
                In his early papers in the 80s,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere"} Lawvere
                emphasized the existence of a contractible
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+quadruple"} adjoint quadruple
                goes under the name
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sufficiently+cohesive+topos"} sufficiently cohesive topos
                in the later axiomatization (modulo some fineprint).
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %em little topos
            that it defines is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} category of sheaves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(X) := Sh(Op(X))
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+open+subsets"} category of open subsets
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/etale+space"} etale space
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            itself is incarnated as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mo *
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = * \in Sh(X)
          %p
            On the other hand, a
            %em big topos
            in which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is incarnated is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} category of sheaves
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            of test spaces with which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            may be probed. For instance for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C =
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
            with their standard
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(C)
            is such a big topos. See for instance,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+topos"} topological topos
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-topos"} quasi-topos
            of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasitopological+space"} quasitopological space
          %p
            In good cases, the intrinsic properties of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            do not depend on whether one regards it as a little topos or as an object of a gros topos. For instance at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            in the section
            %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/cohomology#NonabelianSheafCohomology"} Nonabelian sheaf cohomology with constant coefficients
            it is discussed how the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paracompact+space"} paracompact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with constant coefficients gives the same answer in each case.
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+geometry"}> functorial geometry
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+and+quantity"} space and quantity
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+site"}> topological site
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+truth"} continuous truth
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sufficiently+cohesive+topos"} sufficiently cohesive topos
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} infinitesimal cohesive (infinity,1)-topos
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quality+type"} quality type
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tendue"} étendue
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+decidable+topos"} locally decidable topos
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The notion of a
            %em gros topos
            of a
            %em topological space
            is due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean+Giraud"} Jean Giraud
          %ul
            %li#SGA4
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M.+Artin"}> M. Artin
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A.+Grothendieck"} A. Grothendieck
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+L.+Verdier"} J. L. Verdier
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Théorie des Topos et Cohomologie Etale des Schémas (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SGA4"}> SGA4
                  )
              %strong 269
              (1972). (exposé IV, 2.5 pp.316-318, 4.10 pp.358-365)
          %p In this context see also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+Mac+Lane"}> S. Mac Lane
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"} I. Moerdijk
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sheaves+in+Geometry+and+Logic"} Sheaves in Geometry and Logic
              %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4612-0927-0"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4612-0927-0
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            In the context of a discussion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/big+Zariski+topos"} big Zariski topos
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere76"} Lawvere (1976, p. 110)
            calls the gros-petit distinction ‘
            %em> a surprising twist of logic that is not yet fully clarified
            ’:
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere76
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Variable quantities and variable structures in topoi
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alex+Heller"} Alex Heller
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Myles+Tierney"} Myles Tierney
              (eds.),
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Algebra, Topology and Category Theory – A Collection of Papers in Honor of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Eilenberg"} Samuel Eilenberg
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/C2013-0-10841-0"} doi:10.1016/C2013-0-10841-0
          %p
            The suggestion that a
            %em general notion
            of gros topos is needed goes back to some remarks in
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pursuing+Stacks"} Pursuing Stacks
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere86
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Categories of spaces may not be generalized spaces, as exemplified by directed graphs
              %strong XX
              (1986) 179-186, reprinted as: Reprints in Theory and Applications of Categories,
              %strong 9
              (2005) 1-7 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/9/tr9abs.html"}> tac:tr9
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            “Axiom 0” (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+topos"}> locality
            ) used in
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere86"} Lawvere 1986
            for gros toposes is argued in
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere94"} Lawvere 1994
            to be essentially an insight due to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Cantor"} Georg Cantor
            and is called the
            %em Cantorian Contrast
            (namely between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+spaces"} discrete spaces
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codiscrete+spaces"} codiscrete spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#LawvereRosebrugh03"} Lawvere &amp; Rosebrugh (2003)
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://patryshev.com/books/Sets%20for%20Mathematics.pdf#page=260"} p. 245
          %ul
            %li#LawvereRosebrugh03
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Rosebrugh"} Robert Rosebrugh
              %a{:href => "http://patryshev.com/books/Sets%20for%20Mathematics.pdf#page=260"} p. 245
              in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets+for+Mathematics"} Sets for Mathematics
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511755460"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511755460
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.mta.ca/~rrosebru/setsformath/"} book homepage
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://patryshev.com/books/Sets%20for%20Mathematics.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The axioms 0 and 1 for
            %em toposes of generalized spaces
            given in
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere86"} Lawvere 1986
            later became called the axioms for a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere94
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohesive+Toposes+and+Cantor%27s+%22lauter+Einsen%22"} Cohesive Toposes and Cantor's "lauter Einsen"
              %strong 2
              1 (1994) 5-15 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1093/philmat/2.1.5"}> doi:10.1093/philmat/2.1.5
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/LawvereCohesiveToposes.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            together with axiom 2 they make out a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sufficiently+cohesive+topos"} sufficiently cohesive topos
          %p Further discussion of this axiomatics for gros toposes is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"}> Bill Lawvere
              ,
              %em Categories of space and quantity
              in: J. Echeverria et al (eds.),
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Space of mathematics
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mattearnshaw/lawvere/master/pdfs/1992-categories-of-space-and-quantity.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            where a proposal for a general axiomatization of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            = succeed "-like" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
            = succeed "-like" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
          %p The following two papers contain Lawvere’s early view of a trichotomy between big toposes vs. étendue and locally decidable toposes as paradigmatic “generalized spaces” with “infinitesimally cohesive” in between, with the latter subsumed into the fine structure of cohesion in more recent versions
          %ul
            %li#Law89a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F.+W.+Lawvere"}> F. W. Lawvere
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Qualitative Distinctions between some Toposes of Generalized Graphs
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categories in Computer Science and Logic
                %strong 92
                (1989) 261-299 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1090/conm/092"}> doi:10.1090/conm/092
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://github.com/mattearnshaw/lawvere/raw/master/pdfs/1989-qualitative-distinctions-between-some-toposes-of-generalized-graphs.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Law91a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F.+W.+Lawvere"}> F. W. Lawvere
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Some+Thoughts+on+the+Future+of+Category+Theory"} Some Thoughts on the Future of Category Theory
                %strong 1488
                (1991).
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            in a cohesive topos is also mentioned in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"}> Bill Lawvere
              ,
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/8/tr8.pdf#page=14"} page 14
              of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Taking categories seriously
              %strong XX
              (1986) 147-178, Reprints in Theory and Applications of Categories,
              %strong 8
              (2005) 1-24. &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/8/tr8abs.html"}> tac:tr8
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.emis.de/journals/TAC/reprints/articles/8/tr8.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Under the term
            %em categories of cohesion
            these axioms are discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"}> Bill Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Axiomatic cohesion
              %strong 19
              3 (2007) 41-49 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/19/3/19-03abs.html"}> tac:19-03
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/19/3/19-03.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Another definition of gros vs petit toposes and remarks on applications in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+theory"} Galois theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              Nick Duncan,
              %em Gros and petit toposes
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.cheng.staff.shef.ac.uk/pssl88/pssl88-duncan.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p and yet another one is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spectral+Algebraic+Geometry"} Spectral Algebraic Geometry
              = succeed "-Topoi\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.harvard.edu/~lurie/papers/SAG-rootfile.pdf"} pdf
          %p There is also something relevant in this article:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mathieu+Anel"}> Mathieu Anel
                ,
                %em Grothendieck topologies from unique factorization systems
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0902.1130"}> arXiv:0902.1130
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mamuka+Jibladze"}> Mamuka Jibladze
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Homotopy types for “gros” toposes
                %a{:href => "http://www.rmi.ge/~jib/pubs/thesis.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Calibrated Toposes
                %strong 19
                5 (2012) 889-907. &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.bbms/1354031555"}> euclid:1354031555
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            A discussion and comparison of big vs little approaches to
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2010/10/cohesive_toposes.html"} Cohesive (∞,1)-toposes
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2010/10/petit_1toposes.html"} Petit (∞,1)-toposes
        .revisedby
          %p
